From ff89bd05498bc699dab14e7b6cbabfd9db40fbe1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <assistant@example.com>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 04:03:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Make TTL kill-clock tie-in visible in train.yml

Previously the TTL kill-clock existed only as a resource declaration in
infra_ml_compute/__main__.py - nothing in train.yml itself referenced it,
so reading the pipeline top to bottom gave no indication it existed.

- Add a comment at the provisioning step explaining the TTL schedule is
  created as a side effect of that same `pulumi up`, not a separate step.
- Add a 'Report TTL Kill-Clock' step that reads the ttl_destroy_at stack
  output and writes it to the job summary, so the destroy time is visible
  per-run rather than only discoverable by reading __main__.py.
- Surface the same destroy time in the Slack crash alert, since it directly
  affects what an engineer inspecting a held-open crashed node needs to
  know: the failure trap's hold-open state is not exempt from the TTL - if
  cleanup.yml isn't run before the TTL fires, the node (and its forensic
  NVMe state) gets reclaimed anyway.
- README: document this TTL/failure-trap interaction explicitly in the
  TTL Kill-Clock section (6a), since it wasn't called out anywhere before.
